rqrr, an ordinary dependency

rqrr, by Wolfgang Ziegler (https://github.com/WanzenBug/rqrr), which reads
QR codes and is what `src-tauri/src/qr.rs` is written around.

Licensed "(MIT OR Apache-2.0) AND ISC". The ISC half covers the detection and
decoding routines it carries from quirc, by Daniel Beer, so the choice the
first half offers does not extend to the whole crate and the ISC terms apply
alongside whichever of MIT or Apache-2.0 is taken.

Named here for that reason rather than because it is unusual: every other
Rust dependency is plain MIT or Apache-2.0 and needs no notice, and this one
would look like them in a licence scan that read only the first clause.

Taken with `default-features = false`, which drops its `img` feature. That
feature exists to read a picture out of a file, and Sill already has the
pixels in hand from a screen capture or from the clipboard, so the default
would have pulled in a whole image-decoding crate to do work that is already
done.
